{
  "strategic_goal": "Grant the excise tax, the compensation fund, and a real verifier, then isolate limited liability immunity as the clause that fails. Set the debate on who gets paid, who still faces a lawsuit, and who writes the tier that buys the waiver — not on whether the software saves or ends anyone.",
  "core_thesis": "A mandatory tax and a no-fault fund can pay injured people; immunity cannot be the price of that fund, because the motion's own power-disparity premise already makes unlimited exposure hard to prove and collect, and a compliance-purchased waiver would lock that failure in.",
  "argument_structure": [
    {
      "point": "The chill story dies on the motion's own disparity sentence: if tech giants already outmatch individuals, unlimited liability is mostly a right people cannot use, not the thing blocking beneficial deployment or redress.",
      "evidence": "Text-internal only. The motion asserts both 'current unlimited liability' as the chill and 'the power disparity between tech giants and individuals' as a fact. No filing, proof, or collection series is in the materials — demand that share rather than inventing plaintiff win-rates. If ordinary tort doctrines already pierce the black box, the waiver is a step backward; if they fail, the fix is shifting the burden and a path a person can actually win, not extinguishing the claim for firms that clear a tier.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "sit-166"
    },
    {
      "point": "Treat the VICP model as channeling, not as proof that a fund requires a shield. An excise tax plus a no-fault forum can be defended; pairing that tax with manufacturer protection that pulls covered claims out of ordinary court is a different statute.",
      "evidence": "Public structure of the National Childhood Vaccine Injury Act: covered claims leave ordinary civil court for special masters, an injury table, and an excise tax tied to manufacturer liability protection. Do not cite payout totals, denial rates, or caps — none are in the materials. Compare claimant control and appeal rights, not an invented verdict that VICP 'failed.' Preempt the insurance counter up front: a mandatory pool can sit alongside strict and product liability; this motion sells coverage as the price of limited liability immunity. A new forum is not illegitimate because it is new; it is illegitimate if it immunizes developers.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-275"
    },
    {
      "point": "Hardware telemetry and cryptographic audit trails can be real. Verification of compliance is still not compensation. The live question is authorship: who writes 'state of the art safety practices,' do developers sit on that body, and does certification stop an injured person from suing?",
      "evidence": "The topic text creates the tier and the verifier, plus a board with subpoena power that would both certify the immunity tier and run no-fault payouts. No capture-rate study or standards-body roster is in the materials — do not name a captured body. Well-resourced firms shape the standards later treated as objective knowledge. Costly compliance plus a purchased waiver is incumbents pulling up the drawbridge; answering that means rejecting this cure, not pretending the drawbridge cannot be built. Same board certifying and paying is how compliance becomes theater.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "sit-278"
    },
    {
      "point": "Concede unwanted variability in professional judgment. Deny that average accuracy is a warrant for immunity when injury falls unevenly.",
      "evidence": "Kahneman, Sibony, and Sunstein for unwanted variability — conceded, not mocked. Human costs in hiring, credit scoring, and criminal justice are the domains where disparate outcomes are documented; confidence is 0.72 and these materials supply no effect sizes, so quote none. A no-fault table keyed to aggregate noise reduction buries subgroup injury for covered firms. Lack of human-like intent is a reason to judge outcomes and keep developers on the hook, not a reason to immunize them once a tier is met. Ask which injury table, if any, records uneven error rather than average accuracy.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "skp-beliefs-147"
    }
  ],
  "framing_choices": [
    {
      "frame": "Translate every clause into a person and a check: who is hurt, who pays, whose lawsuit is extinguished, who still faces unlimited exposure if they skip the tier.",
      "why": "Refuses utopia and apocalypse as the stakes. Matches the example ceiling — medical error, discriminatory decisions, consumer injury — and keeps non-compliant developers outside coverage, which the motion already concedes. Success is collection and labor on the balance sheet, not a faster adoption rate bought by killing claims."
    },
    {
      "frame": "Split the bargain in the first minute: tax and fund on one side, limited liability immunity on the other. Judge the forum by whether ordinary injured people recover, not by whether a board exists.",
      "why": "Preempts the collective-insurance counter and the nirvana trap. A fund that leaves liability in place is a different proposal from a fund that purchases a waiver. Opponents cannot paint opposition to immunity as opposition to compensation, research, or audit capacity."
    }
  ],
  "anticipated_challenges": [
    "ATTACK: Strongest hit on the fund-versus-waiver split (skp-beliefs-275) and on the black-box doctrines (skp-beliefs-273): channeling is the compensation. Ordinary tort against an opaque system fails on causation even if res ipsa and product-defect theory exist on paper; discovery costs make the courthouse a rich person's remedy; shells and liability shields already make collection theoretical. Without immunity as the carrot, firms will not pay a tax large enough to fund claims and will not submit to hardware telemetry. A board with subpoena power and an injury table is how a person denied a loan or harmed by a clinical tool gets a check. Defending unlimited liability in name defends a right the people you claim to protect cannot use.",
    "ATTACK: Strongest hit on the noise concession (skp-beliefs-147) and on sit-166: if auditable systems cut unwanted variability, keeping unlimited exposure on compliant firms leaves noisier human judgment in clinics, hiring, and credit, and the same subgroups absorb that harm. Immunity for firms that clear a verified tier is the price of getting the less-noisy system deployed. Burden-shifting inside tort does not build the fund, the audit capacity, or the table.",
    "ASSUMPTION: Load-bearing premise — unlimited liability is largely theoretical today because of power and information disparity, so it is weak evidence of a deployment chill. Contestable if insurers are already pricing open-ended AI exposure in a way that keeps safer systems off the market, or if a non-trivial share of claims is filed, proved, and collected. If that picture is wrong, the text contradiction weakens and the argument has to be remade on collection design, not on the motion contradicting itself. Handle by asking for that share; do not invent it.",
    "ASSUMPTION: Legislative separability — Congress can pass the tax, the fund, and the verifier without the immunity clause. Contestable as a package-deal fact: industry may accept telemetry and the excise only if the waiver is attached. If the package is indivisible, opposing immunity can mean opposing the only fund that passes. Do not compare this exchange to perfect recovery. Compare it to ordinary tort, insurance, and a fund that does not waive liability. A public enforcer that surfaces diffuse injury without selling immunity stays on the table as the alternative, not as a utopia."
  ]
}

